course: dictionary
lesson: 10581
title: Undercharge: when the price is too low
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To undercharge means to ask someone to pay less than the correct price, often by mistake. The shop has not charged enough. «Honesty is the other side of money: a stall undercharges Gran by £2.» The stall asked for £2 less than it should have.
explain: Look for the clues. The word undercharges tells you the price was too low, and the words by £2 tell you how much was missed. If a stall should charge £10 but charges £8, it has undercharged you by £2.
explain: Honest people tell the seller when they have been undercharged. Gran does this, even though nobody would have noticed. Doing the right thing is the honest choice.
fact: Undercharge is a verb: undercharge, undercharges, undercharged, undercharging.
fact: To undercharge means to ask someone to pay less than the real or correct price.
fact: «Undercharge someone by £2» means the price was £2 too low.
fact: «Be undercharged» means you were charged too little, often by mistake.
fact: To undercharge for your work means to ask too little money for it.
exercise: «Honesty is the other side of money: a stall undercharges Gran by £2.» What does undercharges mean? | Asks her to pay more than the correct price | Closes the stall early | *Asks her to pay less than the correct price | Gives her £2 as a present
exercise: A cake should cost £6, but the shop charges £4. By how much has the customer been undercharged? | £4 | £6 | £10 | *£2
exercise: Which person is being honest about being undercharged? | Someone who keeps quiet and walks away with the bargain | Someone who asks for a bigger price | *Someone who tells the shop it charged too little | Someone who hides the bill
test: Which word is the opposite of undercharge? | Pay | Refund | Undercharge | *Overcharge
test: A shop undercharges you when it | *asks for less money than the right price | closes early | gives you a refund | asks for more money than the right price
exam: Mia's shop undercharges her by 50p. What does this mean? | She pays 50p more than the correct price | She gets 50p change | *She pays 50p less than the correct price | She buys 50 sweets
